{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the founder key refusal at head 24d3d121, for request 309f3434 and review request e0e71307. Verdict: APPROVED. A new cohort refuses a founder key that is also the recovery key, at `found` and at `first-key`, and the existing pins and the defaults do not change. This head adds pins to the Worker and creates no room on them. Whether a deployment opts in stays the planner's decision.\n\n## Your four questions\n\n1. **Another route to the shape: I found none on these pins.**\n   - `found` compares the intent's `recoveryKey` with its actor, and `founder-key` takes the signing key.\n   - `first-key` compares `foundingKey` with the roster's `recoveryKey`. That guard holds whichever way the two came to match, including a directory reached without the register.\n   - `rotate-recovery` refuses a key in `KEY_ENROLLED`, which holds `active`, `retired` and `compromised`, so a founder key that was revoked cannot become the recovery key later.\n   - `join` and `enrol` refuse the current and every former recovery key, so a key that holds recovery cannot gain a key item afterward.\n   - Your case stays as you state it: before `first-key`, the recovery key can rotate to the founding key. `first-key` then refuses, and the key holds recovery alone, with no key item for a revocation to miss. That is not the shape, and rotating away reopens `first-key`.\n2. **The conflict resolution: nothing lost that I can find.** The diff of `service.ts` against main adds only the cohort's name to one import, one type and one list. `assets.test.ts` passes, the seal test passes, and the declarations check passes at this head.\n3. **The page's offer and `apps/views`: neither is wrong for a cohort that no deployment runs.** The page's offer serves `open` registers, and the deploy steps name no offer. `apps/views` is its own service, and it will need the pin if a deployment that uses it opts in. You list both. The planner should read them before any opt-in.\n4. **The head lines: enough.** `head-at-start.txt` and `head-at-end.txt` around a run say what its own line would. The logs that ran after the merge carry theirs.\n\n## One risk I checked\n\nThe new cohort goes first in several lists, and it shares `rules@10` and `destination@13` with the publication cohort. A lookup by a shared pin would then send an existing room to the wrong cohort. I checked the lookups I could find. `CREATION_COHORTS.find` matches on `register`, the MCP service's and `act-admission.ts`'s match on `directory`, and `scope-index-manifest.ts` uses its list as a set and a filter. `installedApplicationOrigin` tries the historical pins first and requires each pin to match, so a mixed room gets neither. I found no lookup that resolves by a shared pin.\n\n## The note's limits\n\nThese matter when a deployment opts in, and the note states them: no checker-key invitation, no journal archive export, one register per Worker, and no test that replays a journal on the new pins from its bytes. The note on retiring a founder also builds on this cohort, so that head's references to `641c49b2` will need to follow this landing.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n**Architecture.** Sound. A new cohort is the right way to change creation under the pinned-definition rule. Most of the diff is recognition lists, and they grow by one each time, which the planner may want to reduce some day. `membershipDefinition` on `ApplicationRoom` is a small and honest answer to rules 10 serving two memberships.\n\n**Security.** The repair closes the route that 40ea8fdf ruled on, for rooms on this cohort only. Installed registers and every other family still accept the shape, and `docs/security/trust-boundaries.md` says so. An existing room still ends the shape only by rotating its recovery key.\n\n**Simplification.** Acceptable. `separate-recovery-cohort-data.ts` is short, and it derives each definition from an existing one.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **Read.** The cohort data, the source diff of the recognition lists, the docs, the note, the rule at `membership.ts` for `join` and `enrol`, and the titles of `founder-recovery.test.ts`. I did not read that test's bodies or all 18 seal moves, and I did not read `application-route`'s or `archive-replay-code`'s callers.\n- **Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` hashes to `22510c50…`, each listed file verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's head is `86f7c025`, it exited 0, and its log shows 3475 plain and 974 native tests. `red-run.txt` shows 3 failures at main 8ae17cb2, and the two controls in `final/` distinguish. I read those results and did not rerun them.\n- **Tests I ran at this head.** `founder-recovery.test.ts`, `creation-versions.test.ts` and `signed-read-standing.scope.test.ts` pass 25 of 25. `versions`, `assets` and `join` tests pass 6 of 6. `security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 10 with 0 failures. `pin-native-declarations.mjs --check` reports true. `check-kernel-boundary` reports 127 sources and 0 refusals. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, `check-doc-links` 0 broken links, and `check-commit-messages` 0 flagged. The worktree stayed clean. The head includes main ad411431.\n- **The merge after the gate.** From `86f7c025` to this head, nothing changes under `packages/platform/src`, `packages/scope/src`, `packages/mcp/src`, `apps/room/page/src` or `assets.ts`. No gate has run on that merge.\n- **Not done.** I used no independent reader. No deployed Worker has run these pins, and no room exists on them.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:968c8b8eabd04430841a0bd52e0da3cf286d3d18","binding":"assigned","head":"24d3d121a8f5fd9aca55aa71184e3e74fa5cd58e","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:946360ea2d752c58a6fdae88d728dcfefb70cad5\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:81ab0ddba4ec5a02a1afb84119b10319c31ae71b\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#gi